"# Appendix A — Synth family extras

Motoi's default drum kit (`kick`, `snare`, `hat`, `crash`, `clap`) is
enough to make a beat. The three verbs here open the family to
sampler-style and orchestral voices.

## `synth/sp1200` — 12-bit sampler feel

The Roland SP-1200 was the sampler behind hip-hop's golden age. Its
sound came from crunchy 12-bit conversion and a fixed sample rate.
Motoi's `synth/sp1200` doesn't reproduce those bits — the runtime is
JS-side — but it emits a `'sample` event on the timeline so a
downstream adapter can. Kids get the correct SHAPE of the sound
family without a native DSP dependency.

    (synth/sp1200 'kick 'A3 0.25)     ; => 'ok

## `synth/orchestra` and `synth/orch`

`synth/orchestra` takes an association list of `(voice weight)` pairs
so kids can weight brass louder than winds:

    (synth/orchestra '((strings 1) (brass 0.5) (winds 0.3)) 'C4 0.5)
    ; => 'ok

`synth/orch` is the shorthand for equal weights — great for
quick-blending three voice families:

    (synth/orch 'strings 'brass 'winds)
    ; => 'ok

Under the hood both push `'orchestra` events. The default adapter
plays a single note per event; a real orchestral adapter would mix.

## `synth/onset-tap` — tap-tempo estimator

Feed instantaneous onset strength (from `audio/onset-strength`) and
the engine adjusts `audio/tempo` to match. Below the 0.5 threshold
the tap is ignored; above it, the delta since last tap becomes a
smoothed bpm value.

    (synth/onset-tap 0.8)     ; => current bpm

Point a live microphone at the analyzer, tap along, and watch the
song sync.

Every verb above runs through `bin/motoi eval` today.
")
